{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>In the multitude of people is the king's glory; but in the lack of people is the ruin of the ruler</b> (Mishlei 14:28)." "<b>King Shelomo,</b> peace be upon him, explained in this verse (Mishlei 14:28) that the multitude of the people is the king's glory and honor, for among his men there are mighty ones and sages equal to him or above him; but his superiority and exaltation over the other princes and officers lies in the multitude of his hosts and the breadth of his kingdom and dominion, and this is the reason he is superior and more splendid than them all. And when the people fail him and separate from him, this is the ruin of the master. And the word רזון is from the root רוזנים (rulers). And according to what it said 'the king's glory' (הדרת מלך), it should have said 'the ruin of the king' (מחתת מלך); but it comes to say that the lack of people is such a ruin to him that the name of kingship is removed from him and he reverts to being like one of the rulers (רוזנים) who govern a people, and therefore Scripture calls him by the name רוזן and not by the name king, for there is no king without a people. If so, this verse teaches you that the king's security depends on flesh and blood, since his glory and superiority lie in the multitude of the people and his ruin in their absence. For this reason it was not the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, that there be a king among us, because the people's trust is in the king and the king's trust is in his people; but we, the receivers of the Torah, were commanded in it to fear HaShem, as it is written (Deuteronomy 6:13): ״את ה׳ אלהיך תירא,״ and we were also commanded not to fear the Canaanites, as it is written (ibid. 7): ״לא תירא מהם,״ and it is written (ibid. 31): ״אל תיראו ואל תערצו מפניהם.״ And since the Torah forbade us to trust in flesh and blood and commanded us that every one of us place his trust in HaShem, blessed be He, alone — and when we fulfill this we will not need any man, and every one of Israel will be more secure than the king. But because it was revealed and known before Him, exalted be He, that Israel were destined to ask for themselves a king like the other nations that have kings and rulers, therefore He established for them in the Torah a positive commandment that concedes to them to appoint a king over themselves after they sin in asking for one, and that is what is written (Deuteronomy 17): ״ואמרת אשימה עלי מלך ככל הגוים אשר סביבותי, שום תשים עליך מלך.״ And it would have been fitting that this be reckoned to them as a sin, since the Holy One, blessed be He, made their case extraordinary in the days of their going out from the land of Egypt beyond all the peoples, and exalted their portion above every nation and tongue, to be King over them with His Shekhinah in their midst; and therefore it was grievous in the eyes of the Holy One, blessed be He, when they asked for themselves a king, and its end proved its beginning, for Sha'ul perished in his end. Therefore King Shelomo, peace be upon him, said here: ברב עם הדרת מלך, and we learn from this verse that the righteous man who trusts in HaShem, blessed be He, is more secure than the king, for the king places the main of his trust in his people — if so, it is fitting that the one who trusts in the Holy One, blessed be He, be more secure than the one who trusts in the people, for a king who trusts in the people alone and not in the Holy One, blessed be He, sometimes falls into the hand of his enemies, and all his hosts do not avail or deliver him. And so we find with Sihon and Og, who trusted in the multitude of their hosts and came against Mosheh and Israel, and it availed them nothing — for harm and deliverance from Him is not dependent on the multitude of people but on the decree of HaShem, for sometimes deliverance is not in the multitude of people, as it is written (Psalms 33:16): ״אין המלך נושע ברב חיל גבור לא ינצל ברב כח.״ And it is well known that there were mighty kings, powerful in valor, with fortified cities and hosts like the sand upon the seashore, and when they were all destroyed and fell by the hand of Mosheh, therefore Balak king of Moav was afraid, since his mighty men in whom he sheltered had already died, and he feared for his life because of what Israel had done to the Amorite — and this is what is written." "<b>And Balak son of Tzippor saw all that Israel had done to the Amorite.</b> Balak son of Tzippor was king of Moav, for so it will mention shortly: ״ובלק בן צפור מלך למואב בעת ההיא,״ and if so, it should have said: 'And Balak son of Tzippor king of Moav saw.' And it is possible to explain that here Scripture reveals to us his great fear over the loss of those two kings, Sihon and Og; therefore Scripture explained that from the day he saw all that Israel had done to the Amorite, he no longer regarded himself as a king, and for this reason Scripture mentioned him by his name without title, not by the title king. And ״בעת ההיא״ — 'at that time' — means at the time the Amorite was destroyed, that is, when they died; but after they died he no longer regarded himself as a king. For the Amorite referred to is the known one — Sihon and Og — and there were none like them among the kings of Canaan, as it is written (Amos 2:9): ״ואנכי השמדתי את האמורי מפניהם אשר כגובה ארזים גבהו וחסון הוא כאלונים.״ A parable: to a king who stationed guards to protect him from an army and trusted in them because they were mighty; the army passed through and killed them, and he was terrified for himself. So too Balak saw what was done to Sihon and Og, whom he used to hire for wages to protect him, and they were reckoned equal to all of them, as it is said (Psalms 135:11): ״לסיחון מלך האמורי ולעוג מלך הבשן ולכל ממלכות כנען״ — therefore ״ויגר מואב.״" "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״וירא בלק״ — all the seeing of the wicked is for destruction. ״וירא בלק״ brought Bila'am the wicked, who came to uproot an entire nation. (Genesis 9:22) ״וירא חם אבי כנען,״ (ibid. 6) ״ויראו בני האלהים את בנות האדם,״ (ibid. 12) ״ויראו אותה שרי פרעה,״ (ibid. 34) ״וירא אותה שכם בן חמור.״"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:11.102113818Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}